; Water — the Water terrain tile (emits `water`). FIRST REAL SURFACE for this terrain
; (2026-08-16); previously a flat opaque grey-box slab. Human complaint: "give some
; indication of water [rather] than just a flat blue color."
;
; art.md's own plan for Water was always "surface is a shader/plane, no model needed" —
; this is that plan: a subdivided BoxMesh (8x8 top-face subdivisions, so the vertex-ripple
; shader below has geometry to displace — a flat unsubdivided box has only 4 top verts and
; would show no ripple) with water_surface.gdshader
; (project/assets/shaders/water_surface.gdshader) applied via ShaderMaterial. That shader
; gives a depth-tinted colour gradient (deep vs. shallow, fresnel-driven), a fresnel rim
; brighten, and a gentle animated double-sine ripple — see the shader file's own header
; for the full per-uniform reasoning. No third-party asset is used or needed; nothing here
; requires an attribution entry.
;
; RECESSED OFFSET KEPT FROM THE GREY-BOX (translation y=-0.14 vs. every other terrain's
; -0.1): the retired placeholder Water.tscn dropped its slab 0.04 below the standard tile
; height so a water tile reads as slightly recessed relative to dry land — a free
; legibility win the human never objected to, and worth keeping now that the surface is
; real. Same BoxMesh footprint (0.94x0.94) as every other terrain slab.
;
; NOT DONE (explicitly optional per the task brief): Lilypad/reeds edge dressing from
; Nature Pack. The water surface treatment itself was the must-have; edge dressing was
; flagged as a nice-to-have and was not pursued in this pass to keep scope to the six
; terrains' must-have items. FLAG FOR HUMAN SIGN-OFF: whether the shader reads as "water"
; at the fixed ~45 camera pitch, and every uniform value in water_surface.gdshader.
;
; SLAB WIDENED 0.94 -> 1.0 (2026-08-16, human-reported cross-tile seam gap) — same fix,
; same reasoning as CommonTree1.tscn's header. X/Z only. The Y offset (-0.14, the
; deliberate 0.04-below-standard recess kept from the retired grey-box) is UNCHANGED —
; this fix is X/Z only, and the recess is explicitly called out above as staying as-is.
;
; BOX -> PLANE (2026-08-16, human-reported: "water still shows the edges... when next to a
; water tile itself it should be clear"). Root cause: BoxMesh has 4 vertical SIDE faces,
; not just a top surface. Widening X/Z to 1.0 closed the sky-through-the-gap seam (the
; land-terrain seam bug) but did nothing for water-to-water, because two adjacent water
; boxes' abutting side walls are still real lit geometry — the fresnel/specular response on
; those vertical faces reads as a rim/edge at every tile boundary, including water-water,
; where nothing should be visible at all between two tiles of the same contiguous surface.
; Fixed by switching to a flat PlaneMesh (no side faces to begin with) sized to the same
; 1.0x1.0 tile footprint, same Y position (-0.14) and subdivision counts, same material —
; only the mesh type changed. Two adjacent water tiles' planes now share a coincident edge
; with zero geometry between them, so nothing is left to catch a highlight. A water tile
; against LAND still shows the intended 0.04 recess (the land tile's own box side wall is
; solid down to y=-0.2, well past the plane's y=-0.14, so the plane meets that wall rather
; than leaving a gap) — that read is unchanged and was never the complaint.
;
; WRAPPED UNDER A NODE NAMED "Slab" (2026-08-16, found while here, not previously reported):
; every other terrain's ground piece lives under a node literally named "Slab" so
; OcclusionFader._collect_fadeable_meshes() (scripts/world/occlusion_fader.gd) exempts it —
; fading the ground would open a hole to see through, not "see past an occluding prop."
; Water.tscn never got this wrapper in the original pass, so until now OcclusionFader would
; have treated the water surface itself as a fadeable occluding prop, inconsistent with
; every other terrain. Added for consistency; no report of this manifesting as a visible
; bug, but there was no test coverage to catch it either (see PR notes).
;
; SUBDIVISIONS DROPPED 8x8 -> NONE (2026-08-16, follow-up to water_surface.gdshader's
; ripple removal — see that file's own header for why the geometric ripple was pulled:
; the human's "lines that fade in one direction, then another" report was the wave shape
; itself, not a bug in it). The 8x8 subdivision existed for exactly one reason — "so the
; vertex-ripple shader has geometry to displace" — and with the ripple gone there is
; nothing left to displace; 81 vertices per water tile for what is now a flat, unmoving
; surface is pure waste at grid scale. Back to a single-quad PlaneMesh (4 verts).
[gd_scene load_steps=3 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Shader" path="res://assets/shaders/water_surface.gdshader" id="1_shader"]

[sub_resource type="PlaneMesh" id="PlaneMesh_surface"]
size = Vector2(1.0, 1.0)

[sub_resource type="ShaderMaterial" id="ShaderMaterial_water"]
shader = ExtResource("1_shader")

[node name="Water" type="Node3D"]

[node name="Slab" type="Node3D" parent="."]

[node name="Surface" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="Slab"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, -0.14, 0)
mesh = SubResource("PlaneMesh_surface")
material_override = SubResource("ShaderMaterial_water")
